  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (through Thursday)...\n\nThe main concern today will again be critical fire weather\nconditions, however, the focus has shifted to southeast Montana\nand portions of Wyoming. High temperatures will be well into the\n90s with minimum daytime humidities in the teens. Winds will be\nbreezy out of the south to southwest, with the highest gusts\naround 25 to 35 mph over the Bighorn mountains and near the \nDakota border. A Red Flag Warning is in effect from 9 AM to 9 PM\nMDT today for these areas. Fire weather conditions remain elevated\nfor the rest of the CWA, with highs in the low 80s to low 90s.\nHumidities will be a little higher and wind gusts a little lower\nthan over southeastern Montana. Take care not to cause a spark!\n\nConditions will remain hot and mostly dry into Saturday but a\nchange to surface winds out of the east should allow minimum\ndaytimes humidities to remain mostly in the 20s and 30s percent. \n\nThe next best chance for precipitation (40-90%) arrives late \nSunday into Monday as an upper level trough moves across the\nregion and PWATs increase to 170-210% of normal. With this, any\nshower or thunderstorm will be capable of producing pockets of\nheavy rain. The arrival of this trough will also allow \ntemperatures to cool, with highs in the 70s over the west to 80s \nover the east by Monday.\n\nInto the middle of next week, the pattern looks to remain\nunsettled. This would allow for temperatures to remain near  \nnormal (70s) and a low chance (10-30%) for daily showers and \nthunderstorms. Archer",
